structuredOutput:
{
  "summary": "A human-in-loop initiative to define and close the remaining gaps between the current Legion IDE implementation and an explicitly approved production target. The work should convert “production complete” into measurable product, release, performance, and operational acceptance criteria before implementation planning.",
  "proposals": [
    {
      "slot": "project.name",
      "value": "Legion IDE",
      "rationale": "The topic and repository context explicitly identify the existing product.",
      "anchor": "problem-framing",
      "confidence": "researched"
    },
    {
      "slot": "project.stack",
      "value": "Rust multi-crate Cargo workspace",
      "rationale": "The supplied repository guidance identifies Cargo workspace gates and active Rust crates.",
      "anchor": "constraints",
      "confidence": "researched"
    },
    {
      "slot": "project.deliveryMode",
      "value": "Human-in-loop, evidence-gated production-readiness program",
      "rationale": "The request requires human guidance, while the repository establishes approval boundaries, standing gates, and evidence-based readiness checks.",
      "anchor": "recommended-next-action",
      "confidence": "researched"
    }
  ],
  "openQuestions": [
    {
      "slot": "project.productionTarget",
      "question": "Does production completion mean an internal production-ready build, an external preview, or stable general availability?",
      "why": "Each target requires different signing, distribution, support, telemetry, and quality thresholds."
    },
    {
      "slot": "project.primaryUsers",
      "question": "Which users and workflows must be reliable at launch?",
      "why": "The current CLI is described as a shell proof rather than the real renderer, so the required end-user experience is not yet defined."
    },
    {
      "slot": "project.v1Scope",
      "question": "Which editor, workspace, agent, AI-provider, tracking, and collaboration capabilities are mandatory for the production target?",
      "why": "Several crates have real behavior, but deferred surfaces remain and no authoritative launch boundary was provided."
    },
    {
      "slot": "project.releaseMatrix",
      "question": "Which channels and operating systems require production installers, signing, notarization, updates, and rollback support?",
      "why": "The release pipeline currently produces dry-run descriptors without production signing, and no hosted release workflow is configured."
    },
    {
      "slot": "project.performanceSLOs",
      "question": "What measurable limits should apply to startup, input-to-paint latency, scrolling, memory, 100K-file workspaces, and 100 MB files?",
      "why": "The existing harness is a deterministic skeleton, while full workloads and the known large-file degradation need explicit acceptance thresholds."
    },
    {
      "slot": "project.qualityPolicy",
      "question": "Must the independent three-OS smoke workflow become merge-blocking before the selected production target?",
      "why": "Promotion requires four consecutive green scheduled runs and owner sign-off, but the current hosted-run history was not available during this exploration."
    },
    {
      "slot": "project.operationalReadiness",
      "question": "What crash reporting, privacy, telemetry, incident response, support, update-recovery, and rollback capabilities are required?",
      "why": "Repository gates cover engineering correctness, but the operational definition of production readiness is unresolved."
    },
    {
      "slot": "project.timeline",
      "question": "What deadline and risk tolerance should govern scope cuts and sequencing?",
      "why": "A preview-first path and a stable-GA path have materially different cost and risk."
    }
  ],
  "notes": [
    {
      "heading": "Problem Framing",
      "body": "This is not a greenfield build. Legion IDE already has a substantial Rust workspace, proposal-mediated saves, projection-only UI boundaries, active indexing/agent/memory/AI crates, and 21 standing phase gates. The central problem is to establish a finite production definition and close only the gaps required by that definition."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Constraints",
      "body": "Planning must remain read-only and must not publish, deploy, release, or alter unrelated files. Existing architectural boundaries must be preserved: UI remains projection-only, saves remain proposal-mediated and fail closed, observability identifiers remain validated, and release descriptors cannot claim a production signer until real signing work lands. Gate existence does not prove current success because no gate suite was run. Local Legion status and map processes could not start because the Windows sandbox returned error 1920, so current roadmap state, exploration history, and hosted-run results remain unverified."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Open Questions",
      "body": "Human intake must settle the production tier, target users, mandatory workflows, release matrix, performance SLOs, deferred-feature boundary, operational requirements, timeline, and whether hosted smoke promotion is a launch prerequisite. These decisions should not be inferred from the presence of code or gates."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Viable Approaches",
      "body": "Conservative: target an external preview, close only critical renderer, packaging, signing, update, and stability gaps, and document limitations; fastest and lowest risk, but not stable GA. Balanced: define a production-readiness contract across product scope, quality, performance, release, security, and operations, then execute phased closure with evidence gates; best balance of usefulness and control. Ambitious: finish all deferred surfaces, full cross-platform distribution, large-workspace performance, hosted gate maturity, and operational infrastructure before launch; most complete but highest schedule and integration risk."
    },
    {
      "heading": "Recommended Next Action",
      "body": "Choose the balanced approach. First conduct a bounded human decision session that produces a production-readiness contract containing launch scope, explicit non-goals, measurable acceptance criteria, evidence owners, required platforms, blocking gates, and permitted deferrals. Then compare the contract against the roadmap and codebase map to create a prioritized gap register; do not begin implementation from the broad phrase “production completion.”"
    },
    {
      "heading": "Start Or Plan Handoff",
      "body": "Use a plan handoff because Legion IDE is an existing project; do not reinitialize it with Legion start. After the open questions are answered and current Legion state is successfully inspected, hand the approved production-readiness contract to `legion plan` as a dedicated completion phase. No start, plan, build, release, or artifact-writing command was run automatically."
    }
  ]
}
